                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In this paper, we determine the complete adjacency spectrum of the line graph  $ L(BL_n(k-1,k)) $ for all  $ n \\geq 2k-1 $. Since  $ BL_n(k-1,k) \\cong BL_n(n-k,n-k+1) $, we may assume without loss of generality that  $ n \\geq 2k-1 $. Our approach rests on two key observations: (i) the spectrum of  $ BL_n(k-1,k) $ can be deduced from that of the Johnson graph  $ J(n,k-1) $, and (ii) the spectrum of the line graph  $ L(BL_n(k-1,k)) $ can then be extracted from the nonzero singular values of the incidence matrix  $ B $ of  $ BL_n(k-1,k) $ via Lemma 2.1.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                570,
                1020,
                771
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    570.0
                ],
                [
                    1020.0,
                    570.0
                ],
                [
                    1020.0,
                    771.0
                ],
                [
                    160.0,
                    771.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "More precisely, we obtain the following. When  $ n \\geq 2k $, the eigenvalues of  $ L(BL_n(k - 1, k)) $ are integers and their multiplicities are expressed in terms of binomial coefficients (Theorem 3.2). When  $ n = 2k - 1 $, the graph  $ BL_n(k - 1, k) $ is itself a distance-transitive regular graph, and its line graph is vertex-transitive with an entirely integral spectrum; we determine this spectrum completely in Theorem 3.4. As a special case (Example 3.3), we recover and complete the spectrum of  $ L(n) $ left open by Mirafzal in [13]: the multiplicities of the distinct eigenvalues  $ n - 1, n - 2, 0, -1, -2 $ of  $ L(n) $ are  $ 1, n - 1 $,  $ \\frac{n(n - 3)}{2}, n - 1 $, and  $ \\frac{(n - 1)(n - 2)}{2} $, respectively.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                773,
                1022,
                1014
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
